> ## If They Choose the Rootway (continued)
> 
> The basin’s water reflects the question back at each hero. They do not need to give up an item—only answer honestly.
> 
> Ask:
> 
> - **Ekemon:** “What are you willing to give up in order to become a Titan?”
> - **Varkash:** “What do you protect when strength alone cannot solve a problem?”
> - **Dru:** “When nature is wounded, when do you heal—and when do you let it change?”
> 
> There is no wrong answer, but the temple can tell whether they mean it.
> 
> ### Resolution
> 
> If the players answer thoughtfully:
> 
> - Roots unwind from the far door.
> - Each hero gains **1 Rootway Favor**: once before reaching the Heartshard, they can add **1d4** to a saving throw, skill check, or attack roll.
> - Dru learns an important fact from the vision:
> 
> > “The Heartshard can be moved—but only by someone already touched by Titan power, and only if the bearer accepts a cost. If it is carried without a promise, it will keep calling storms.”
> 
> If someone gives a selfish or cruel answer, the path still opens—but thorny roots scrape them for **1d4 damage**, and the storm grows louder.
> 
> ### What the Vision Also Shows
> 
> Whether they succeed or fail, show them this:
> 
> - Purifier Captain **Marcen** is already near the Heartshard.
> - He has the black iron breaker-hammer.
> - Two Dawn-Knights are with him.
> - Light-Priest **Arlen** is not with them.
> 
> This is important: Marcen is acting more aggressively than the reasonable Church priest may have intended.
> 
> Then the roots open into a lower temple hallway.
> 
> ---
> 
> # SCENE 8 – The Chamber of Broken Weather
> 
> Whether the party took the Hall of Thunder or Rootway, the routes rejoin here.
> 
> Describe:
> 
> - A broad circular room in the middle of the temple.
> - The floor is broken into floating chunks of stone, slowly orbiting a bottomless-looking blue storm below.
> - Rain rises upward from the storm, then becomes mist near the ceiling.
> - Across the room is a huge bronze door marked with a cracked lightning bolt: the final door to the **Heart Chamber**.
> - A narrow route of floating stones crosses the room.
> 
> The Titan Seed pulls Ekemon toward the far door like a magnet.
> 
> ## Challenge: Crossing the Floating Stones
> 
> The platforms move when thunder sounds. The party can cross in many ways.
> 
> ### Standard approaches
> 
> - **Acrobatics, DC 12:** leap and balance between the stones.
> - **Athletics, DC 12:** make a difficult but forceful jump, grab ledges, or carry someone.
> - **Survival or Perception, DC 11:** read the rhythm of thunder and predict when platforms will shift.
> - **Nature, DC 11 (Dru):** recognize the stones move like a storm cycle: gather, strike, release.
> - **Titan command, DC 12 (Ekemon):** ask, demand, or persuade the storm to make a path.
> 
> Encourage teamwork. For example:
> - Varkash anchors a rope.
> - Dru grows vines between two stones.
> - Ekemon uses his shield or weapon to stabilize someone.
> - One player succeeds at a setup check, giving another advantage.
> 
> ### On Success
> 
> They reach the bronze door safely.
> 
> ### On Failure
> 
> A character falls—but catches a lower platform rather than disappearing into the storm.
> 
> A companion can rescue them with:
> - **DC 10 Athletics**
> - A rope
> - Druid vines
> - Any clever spell or ability
> 
> The fallen character takes **1d6 bludgeoning or lightning damage**—their choice depending on the narration—and loses a little time.
> 
> If there have already been noisy failures or delays, let the party hear armored boots approaching behind them: **Marcen’s people are closing in.**
> 
> ---
> 
> # SCENE 9 – The Bronze Door: The Titan’s Last Question
> 
> The bronze door has no handle. In its center is a palm-shaped depression, much too large for a normal person—but exactly where Ekemon feels the Titan Seed tugging.
> 
> As he gets close, a voice rolls through the room like distant thunder:
> 
> > “STORM IS NOT KIND.  
> > STORM IS NOT CRUEL.  
> > STORM CHANGES THE WORLD.  
> > WHAT WILL YOU CHANGE?”
> 
> Ask Ekemon directly what he says.
> 
> Possible answers:
> - “I’ll protect people from the storm.”
> - “I’ll make sure nobody can use this power to hurt others.”
> - “I’ll become strong enough to defend my friends.”
> - “I’ll change things—but I won’t destroy everything.”
> - “I’ll take the power and become a Titan.”
> 
> Any sincere answer opens the door. But it affects how the Heartshard reacts later.
> 
> ### Optional Check
> 
> If he wants to understand rather than merely answer:
> 
> - **Religion, Nature, or Insight, DC 12**
> 
> On success, reveal:
> 
> > The temple does not ask whether Ekemon is good enough. It asks whether he accepts responsibility for the consequences of power.
> 
> The door opens with a long groan.
> 
> ---
> 
> # SCENE 10 – The Heart Chamber
> 
> ## Read-Aloud Description
> 
> Beyond the door is a vast chamber carved into the canyon wall.
> 
> The entire back wall has collapsed away, leaving the room open to the roaring canyon and the ring of storm clouds beyond. A waterfall crashes nearby, but its spray hangs frozen in the air.
> 
> At the center of the chamber floats the **Titan Heartshard**:
> 
> - A crystal the size of a large backpack—or perhaps a small boulder, if you want it more impressive.
> - Blue-white light pulses inside it like a heartbeat.
> - Lightning chains stretch from it into four broken stone pillars around the room.
> - With every pulse, thunder rolls through the canyon.
> 
> Standing on the far side are:
> 
> - **Purifier Captain Marcen**, holding the black iron breaker-hammer.
> - **Two Dawn-Knights**, nervous but armed.
> - Potentially **Scout Tomas**, if the party did not save or persuade him earlier.
> 
> Marcen turns as the party enters.
> 
> > “Of course. The druid and the horned creature came for it.”  
> > “Stand aside. This idol ends today.”
> 
> He points to the Heartshard.
> 
> > “One strike. The false god’s curse ends. The canyon is free.”
> 
> Dru immediately realizes this is untrue—or at least dangerously incomplete.
> 
> > “No. The lightning chains are holding back its pain. Break it now, and the storm will burst outward.”
> 
> Now pause and make the stakes explicit to the players:
> 
> - Marcen believes destroying the Heartshard will end the storm.
> - Dru believes smashing it will cause a catastrophic storm and perhaps collapse the canyon.
> - Ekemon may be able to carry or calm it because of his Titan Seed.
> - The Heartshard is frightened and unstable; it reacts to emotion and violence.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## The First Choice: Talk, Sneak, or Fight
> 
> Ask: **What do you do?**
> 
> ### Option A: Try to Convince Marcen
> 
> Marcen is hardline, but the Dawn-Knights are not necessarily villains.
> 
> A character may address one of three arguments:
> 
> 1. **The danger argument:** “Breaking it will hurt the villages.”  
>    - **Persuasion, DC 13**
>    - Advantage if they learned the Rootway’s truth or calmed the wisps.
>    - On success, one Dawn-Knight lowers their weapon and says:
>      > “Captain… we were ordered to protect people, not gamble with their lives.”
> 
> 2. **The authority argument:** “Light-Priest Arlen did not order this.”  
>    - **Persuasion or Deception, DC 12**
>    - Stronger if they actually met Arlen.
>    - On success, the knights hesitate; Marcen loses their unquestioning support.
> 
> 3. **The moral argument:** “You cannot call yourself pure if you destroy something that is hurting.”  
>    - **Insight, Religion, or Persuasion, DC 13**
>    - On success, Marcen is shaken for a moment, though he may cover it with anger.
> 
> If the party earns **two successes**, one knight refuses to fight and the other has disadvantage on their first attack or may surrender after being hurt.
> 
> Marcen himself probably will not peacefully surrender unless someone gives an especially powerful speech, perhaps combined with a natural 20 or strong previous relationship with Tomas/Arlen.
> 
> ### Option B: Sneak or Disarm
> 
> The room has floating spray, broken columns, and thunder that can hide movement.
> 
> - **Stealth, DC 13:** get close to Marcen or the hammer.
> - **Sleight of Hand, DC 13:** pull or knock away the hammer.
> - **Athletics, DC 12:** shove Marcen away from the Heartshard or grapple him.
> 
> If the hammer is taken, Marcen becomes furious and attacks—but the immediate risk of him shattering the Heartshard is reduced.
> 
> ### Option C: Fight Immediately
> 
> If Ekemon charges, or if conversation fails, begin combat.
> 
> ---
> 
> # SCENE 11 – Battle at the Heartshard
> 
> This should feel dramatic but remain manageable. Use fewer enemies or lower HP if the group is small or young.
> 
> ## Battlefield Features
> 
> At initiative count 20—or once each round after the first—roll or choose one **Storm Surge**:
> 
> 1. **Lightning Flash:** One creature near a metal weapon or armor makes a DC 11 Dexterity save or takes **1d4 lightning damage**.
> 2. **Gale:** Everyone on an exposed edge makes a DC 10 Strength or Dexterity save or is pushed 5 feet.
> 3. **Floating Spray:** The chamber is lightly obscured until the next round; ranged attacks are at disadvantage.
> 4. **Heart Pulse:** Everyone hears a wave of fear. A character can make a DC 11 Wisdom save or be unable to take reactions until next turn. On a success, they instead gain advantage on their next attempt to calm or communicate with the Heartshard.
> 
> Do not make every effect damaging. The Heartshard is distressed, not malicious.
> 
> ## Simple Enemy Stats
> 
> ### Purifier Captain Marcen
> 
> - **AC:** 15  
> - **HP:** 28–35, depending on party strength  
> - **Speed:** 30 ft.  
> - **Sword:** +4 to hit, **1d6 + 2** slashing  
> - **Breaker-Hammer (special):** +4 to hit, **1d8 + 2** bludgeoning  
> - **Fanatical Resolve (once):** When reduced to 0 HP, Marcen can remain at 1 HP instead. On his next turn, he tries to reach and strike the Heartshard unless physically stopped.
> 
> **Marcen’s goal is not primarily to kill the party.** He wants to get to the Heartshard and smash it. Make that clear through his movement and dialogue.
> 
> ### Dawn-Knights (use 1 or 2)
> 
> - **AC:** 14  
> - **HP:** 11 each  
> - **Sword:** +3 to hit, **1d6 + 1** slashing  
> - **Morale:** If Marcen falls, or if one knight is badly hurt, they surrender unless personally fanatical.
> 
> ### Optional: The Heartshard Helps Ekemon
> 
> If Ekemon comforted the wisps, answered the door’s question responsibly, or otherwise has acted protectively, he can use this **once in the encounter**:
> 
> **Titan Echo:** As a bonus action or dramatic action, Ekemon calls to the Heartshard. Make a **DC 12 Persuasion, Intimidation, Religion, or Constitution check**.
> 
> - **Success:** choose one:
