> ## SCENE 11 – Battle at the Heartshard (continued)
> 
> ### Titan Echo — Ekemon Calls to the Heartshard
> 
> If Ekemon has treated the storm spirits kindly, answered the temple’s question with responsibility, or otherwise acted as a protector, he may call on the Heartshard once during the battle.
> 
> As a bonus action—or simply as a dramatic special action—Ekemon says what he wants the storm to do and rolls a **DC 12 Persuasion, Intimidation, Religion, or Constitution check**.
> 
> On a success, choose one effect:
> 
> 1. **Storm Shield**  
>    Lightning curls around Ekemon or an ally. Until the start of Ekemon’s next turn, that character gains **+2 AC**.
> 
> 2. **Thunder Push**  
>    A burst of wind throws Marcen or another enemy backward. The enemy makes a **DC 12 Strength save** or is pushed **10 feet** and falls prone.
> 
> 3. **Lightning Warning**  
>    A lightning bolt strikes the ground beside Marcen rather than hitting him. He must make a **DC 12 Wisdom save** or lose his next reaction and be unable to move closer to the Heartshard until the end of his next turn.
> 
> 4. **Storm’s Truth**  
>    The Heartshard sends a vision to everyone in the room: the canyon collapsing, farms flooding, and nearby villages struck by uncontrolled storms if the crystal is shattered.  
>    The Dawn-Knights must make a **DC 11 Wisdom save**. On a failure, they refuse to help Marcen break the shard.
> 
> On a failure, the Heartshard is too frightened to help. It still creates a dramatic effect—thunder, flying spray, a cracked pillar falling—but it is uncontrolled. Nobody needs to take damage unless that makes the scene more exciting.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Marcen’s Breaking Attempt
> 
> Make Marcen’s intention visible from the beginning:
> 
> > “Stand aside! One clean strike, and this blasphemy ends!”
> 
> If Marcen reaches the Heartshard with the breaker-hammer, he spends an action attempting to smash it.
> 
> The party can stop him by:
> - Reducing him to 0 HP;
> - Grappling, shoving, or disarming him;
> - Convincing him;
> - Blocking his path;
> - Using storm magic or Titan Echo;
> - Having a Dawn-Knight turn against him.
> 
> ### If Marcen Gets One Strike In
> 
> Do **not** have the Heartshard simply explode. Instead, make it a major escalation.
> 
> The hammer cracks against the crystal. A thin black line appears across its blue-white surface.
> 
> Everyone hears a huge, painful thunderclap.
> 
> - Every character makes a **DC 11 Dexterity save**.
>   - Success: no damage, but knocked or staggered.
>   - Failure: **1d6 thunder damage** and pushed 5 feet.
> - The whole temple shakes.
> - One of the lightning chains snaps.
> - The storm outside becomes visibly worse.
> 
> Dru cries:
> 
> > “One more strike could break it loose!”
> 
> Now the battle becomes urgent: they must stop Marcen and stabilize the Heartshard.
> 
> ### If Marcen Succeeds in Breaking It Completely
> 
> This should only happen if the party cannot stop him after multiple chances. It creates a dramatic alternate ending, not a failed adventure.
> 
> The Heartshard bursts into several smaller fragments. A huge storm roar passes through the canyon. The temple begins collapsing, and the heroes must escape with at least one fragment—especially the largest piece, which flies toward Ekemon’s Titan Seed.
> 
> This can become a later quest: the party must recover or rejoin scattered Heartshard fragments before the storm spreads.
> 
> But ideally, for this adventure, Marcen is stopped before the second strike.
> 
> ---
> 
> # SCENE 12 – The Moment After the Battle
> 
> Once Marcen is defeated, disarmed, surrenders, or is restrained, the room becomes strangely quiet.
> 
> The Heartshard still pulses too quickly. The storm is not over.
> 
> Dru examines the four pillars and lightning chains.
> 
> > “The temple’s old bindings are failing. We can’t just leave it like this.”
> 
> She explains the choices plainly:
> 
> > “We have three paths. We can bind the Heartshard here, carry it away, or try to destroy it safely. But each path has a cost.”
> 
> This is the major moral and story choice of the adventure.
> 
> ---
> 
> # SCENE 13 – What To Do With the Titan Heartshard
> 
> ## Option 1: Stabilize It in the Temple
> 
> The heroes repair or renew the four lightning bindings and leave the Heartshard in its ancient chamber.
> 
> ### What it means
> 
> - The canyon storm calms over time.
> - The Heartshard remains dangerous but contained.
> - Druids will guard it.
> - The Human Church may remain suspicious, claiming the “idol” has not truly been dealt with.
> - Ekemon does **not** gain a major new piece of Titan power now—but he proves he can protect power without possessing it.
> 
> ### The Ritual
> 
> Each hero can contribute in a different way:
> 
> - **Ekemon:** place a hand near the Heartshard and promise to guard rather than dominate it.  
>   - **DC 12 Constitution, Religion, or Persuasion**
> 
> - **Dru:** call roots, rain, stone, and the Fourfold Mother’s balance back into the broken pillars.  
>   - **DC 12 Nature** or an appropriate spell
> 
> - **Varkash:** physically reconnect a chain, brace a broken pillar, or hold position against the storm.  
>   - **DC 12 Athletics**
> 
> They need **two successes before two failures**.
> 
> ### Success
> 
> The chains brighten. The Heartshard’s pulse slows from panic to a deep, steady rhythm. The circular storm above the canyon begins to unravel.
> 
> Ekemon sees a final vision:
> 
> - A vast Titan silhouette sleeping beneath cloud and mountain;
> - Its hand open, not clenched;
> - The feeling of: **“Not yet. Grow first.”**
> 
> The Heartshard remains, but Ekemon gains a minor blessing:
> 
> **Storm-Keeper’s Mark:** Once per long rest, Ekemon may gain advantage on one saving throw against lightning, thunder, forced movement, or being frightened.
> 
> ### Failure
> 
> The ritual works imperfectly. The storm weakens, but the Heartshard remains unstable and the temple continues to tremble.
> 
> The druids can keep it contained for now, but this becomes a future problem. Ekemon still receives the Storm-Keeper’s Mark, but the canyon remains a dangerous place.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Option 2: Ekemon Carries the Heartshard
> 
> This is the most “Titan champion” path.
> 
> ### What it means
> 
> Ekemon uses his Titan Seed to draw the Heartshard into himself, or into a special vessel made from his gear—an amulet, weapon head, shield emblem, or stone given by the druids.
> 
> The canyon storm ends much more quickly because the source is no longer trapped in the temple. But Ekemon becomes more visibly linked to Titan power.
> 
> The druids may accept this cautiously if he has earned their trust. The Human Church will be deeply alarmed if they learn of it.
> 
> ### The Choice Before the Ritual
> 
> Ask Ekemon:
> 
> > “Why should you carry it? What promise will keep this power from becoming a weapon against everyone else?”
> 
> Possible promises:
> - “I will use it to protect my friends.”
> - “I will not use it to hurt people just because I am angry.”
> - “I will defend the weak from people who abuse power.”
> - “I will return it if I become dangerous.”
> 
> There is no required answer, but a thoughtful promise makes the ritual safer.
> 
> ### The Ritual
> 
> Ekemon approaches the Heartshard as Dru and Varkash keep the chamber stable.
> 
> - **Ekemon:** DC 13 **Constitution**, **Religion**, or **Persuasion**
> - **Dru:** DC 11 **Nature** to guide the transfer safely
> - **Varkash:** DC 11 **Athletics** or **Intimidation** to hold off storm surges, brace the chamber, or command the battlefield
> 
> They need **two successes before two failures**.
> 
> ### Success
> 
> The Heartshard rises from its chains. Lightning lashes toward Ekemon—but instead of harming him, it coils around his Titan Seed.
> 
> His eyes flash blue-white for a moment. His shadow becomes enormous and Titan-shaped against the wall.
> 
> Then the power settles.
> 
> The storm ring above the canyon breaks open, and clear sunlight pours through.
> 
> #### Reward: Heartshard-Bound
> 
> Ekemon gains a new Titan power. Keep it simple and fun:
> 
> **Heartshard Spark — once per long rest:**  
> When Ekemon hits with a weapon attack, he may deal **+1d6 lightning or thunder damage**. The target must succeed on a **DC 12 Strength save** or be pushed 5 feet.
> 
> Or, if you prefer a defensive power:
> 
> **Titanic Shelter — once per long rest:**  
> As a reaction when Ekemon or a nearby ally takes damage, reduce that damage by **1d6 + Ekemon’s proficiency bonus**, as wind and stone briefly protect them.
> 
> You can let Ekemon choose which style he wants: **storm-striker** or **storm-guardian**.
> 
> #### Cost / Story Consequence
> 
> The Heartshard is now part of him. In moments of strong anger, fear, or excitement:
> - His voice may echo with distant thunder;
> - Static may dance over his horns;
> - Weather may respond slightly to his mood.
> 
> It is not a curse, but it is a responsibility—and a thing the Purifiers may pursue.
> 
> ### Failure
> 
> The Heartshard begins entering Ekemon, but the process is painful and unstable.
> 
> He takes **1d6 lightning damage**—not lethal, just dramatic—and must choose:
> 
> 1. **Pull back:** The Heartshard stays in the temple, and they must stabilize it instead.
> 2. **Keep going:** He absorbs only a partial fragment. The main Heartshard remains, but he gains a weaker version of the power and a future quest to return for the rest.
> 
> For a satisfying ending, I would generally let him succeed unless he actively chooses reckless behavior or the group likes consequences and follow-up quests.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Option 3: Destroy It Safely
> 
> This is possible, but the druids strongly oppose it. It should require care, sacrifice, and a clear reason.
> 
> ### What it means
> 
> The party believes no person or faction should possess the Heartshard, and that its continued existence is too dangerous.
> 
> Dru says:
> 
> > “If it must end, then it must be allowed to end gently—not struck in fear with an iron hammer.”
> 
> ### The Ritual
> 
> They must use the waterfall, the temple’s lightning chains, and natural roots to disperse the power gradually into the land.
> 
> - **Dru:** DC 13 Nature
> - **Ekemon:** DC 12 Constitution, Religion, or Persuasion to persuade the Heartshard to release its power
> - **Varkash:** DC 12 Athletics to position the old chains or redirect a collapsing pillar
> 
> Need **three successes before two failures**.
> 
> ### Success
> 
> The crystal slowly becomes rain, wind, and blue sparks. The power spreads harmlessly:
> - Rain falls across the nearby farms;
> - The river runs clean;
> - Small green shoots appear in the scorched clearing from the journey.
> 
> Ekemon receives a final Titan vision:
> 
> > “A heart may become a world. A world may become a heart.”
> 
> He does not gain the Heartshard as an item, but he earns the respect of the druids and perhaps a smaller permanent Titan blessing.
> 
> **Gift
