**assistant**:
> ### Gift of the Dispersed Storm
> 
> If the party destroys the Heartshard safely, Ekemon gains:
> 
> **Rain-Caller’s Favor — once per long rest:**  
> Ekemon may cast a small storm effect: extinguish normal flames in a 15-foot area, create a strong gust of wind, or cause gentle rain to fall in a small area for up to 10 minutes.
> 
> This is not an attack power. It represents that Ekemon did not *own* the storm; he helped set it free.
> 
> ### Failure
> 
> The Heartshard shatters unevenly. The party must escape the collapsing temple while carrying a large fragment—or several smaller fragments—before the entire canyon comes down around them.
> 
> This gives you a great “escape sequence” and turns the fragment into a future responsibility. But, as above, for a satisfying first visit to the canyon, I would favor allowing the careful ritual to work.
> 
> ---
> 
> # SCENE 14 – Escape from the Sky Temple
> 
> Use this scene if:
> 
> - Marcen damaged the Heartshard;
> - The Heartshard was destroyed or removed;
> - A ritual went poorly;
> - Or you simply want a dramatic finale.
> 
> Even if the ritual succeeded perfectly, the old temple has been held together by ancient magic for centuries. Once its heart changes, it begins to fail.
> 
> Describe:
> 
> > The last lightning chain goes dark.  
> > For one breath, all is silent.  
> > Then the sky-temple groans. Cracks race through the walls. Stone begins to fall into the roaring canyon below.
> 
> Dru shouts:
> 
> > “The old paths are coming down! Run!”
> 
> ## Run It As a Group Skill Challenge
> 
> They need **3 successes before 2 failures**. Each player should describe how they help. Do not allow exactly the same skill twice in a row unless their approach changes.
> 
> Suggested DC: **11 or 12**.
> 
> ### Possible actions
> 
> - **Athletics:** leap gaps, carry someone, force open a jammed door, brace collapsing stone.
> - **Acrobatics:** race across cracked platforms, dodge falling rubble.
> - **Survival / Perception:** spot the safer route through smoke, rain, and broken halls.
> - **Nature (Dru):** call vines to create a handhold, identify stable roots, redirect water.
> - **Persuasion / Intimidation (Ekemon):** command frightened storm spirits or the wind to hold rubble back for a moment.
> - **Constitution:** endure lightning and carry on through the storm.
> - **A spell or clever item:** automatic success, or advantage on the next roll, if it clearly solves a problem.
> 
> ### Failure consequences
> 
> On each group failure, choose one:
> 
> 1. A character takes **1d6 bludgeoning damage** from falling stone.
> 2. The party loses a useful item: rope, torch, shield strap, etc. It can be recovered later.
> 3. They are separated for one short moment: one character must be rescued with a quick **DC 10 Athletics, Acrobatics, or magic** check.
> 4. The escape route changes, forcing them out onto a narrow exterior ledge.
> 
> Avoid killing anyone here. This is an action-movie ending, not a punishment.
> 
> ### Final Escape Image
> 
> They burst out onto the ruined bridge or cliffside path as a section of the temple tower collapses inward. Dust rises from the canyon. Then rain begins to fall normally—not in circles, not upward, not in angry bursts.
> 
> If the Heartshard was stabilized or carried safely, the great storm halo starts to open. Sunlight pierces it in columns.
> 
> ---
> 
> # SCENE 15 – The Canyon Aftermath
> 
> Back at the druid camp, the situation depends on the party’s choices.
> 
> ## Archdruid Lysara’s Response
> 
> ### If they stabilized the Heartshard
> 
> Lysara bows to Ekemon—not as to a king, but as to a fellow guardian.
> 
> > “You had the chance to take power, and chose instead to keep it safe. That is rarer than strength.”
> 
> The druids establish a watch over the restored temple.
> 
> **Campaign consequence:** The Canyon of Storms becomes a druid-held sacred site. The Human Church may still challenge that claim later.
> 
> ---
> 
> ### If Ekemon carries the Heartshard
> 
> Lysara is concerned, but can accept it if Ekemon made a sincere promise and handled the ritual responsibly.
> 
> > “Then you carry more than a weapon. You carry a weather system, a wound, and a sleeping memory. We will help you remember that.”
> 
> Dru may be asked to check in on Ekemon if his storm power begins growing erratic.
> 
> **Campaign consequence:** Ekemon becomes a visible Titan-bound figure. Rumors spread:
> - “A horned child carried lightning out of the canyon.”
> - “The storm chose a champion.”
> - “The Purifiers failed to kill a false god.”
> 
> This can draw allies, curious scholars, frightened villagers, and future hunters.
> 
> ---
> 
> ### If they dispersed the Heartshard safely
> 
> Lysara is sad, but respectful.
> 
> > “The old world is changing. Perhaps this was its way of becoming rain instead of thunder.”
> 
> The druids regard the party as people who can be trusted with terrible choices.
> 
> **Campaign consequence:** The storm is truly gone, but the old temple loses much of its magic. The party may later discover that some of the dispersed Titan power has awakened elsewhere—in a storm-born creature, a sacred tree, or a child with strange lightning dreams.
> 
> ---
> 
> # SCENE 16 – The Human Church’s Response
> 
> Even though Ekemon did not speak to the Human Church first, their response matters.
> 
> ## Light-Priest Arlen
> 
> Arlen arrives with a few soldiers after the crisis, perhaps having learned that Marcen went rogue.
> 
> He sees the damage, the changed weather, and any captured or wounded Dawn-Knights.
> 
> Arlen is serious, tired, and morally unsettled—not a cartoon villain.
> 
> > “Captain Marcen was sent to secure the site, not gamble with the lives of every village below it.”
> 
> If the party saved Tomas or any Dawn-Knights, they speak up for Ekemon:
> 
> > “They could have left us.”  
> > “The tiefling stopped the captain from breaking it.”  
> > “The druid was right.”
> 
> Arlen thanks them, even if he remains uneasy about Titan power.
> 
> ### If Ekemon carries the Heartshard
> 
> Arlen says:
> 
> > “I cannot call this safe. But I have seen enough today to know that a hammer in frightened hands is not safety either.”
> 
> He will not attack. But he may report the matter to higher Church authorities, creating a future political complication.
> 
> ### If the Heartshard remains or was dispersed
> 
> Arlen may negotiate:
> - The Church withdraws its soldiers for now;
> - The druids agree to warn villages of future magical dangers;
> - The party is recognized as the group that prevented a disaster.
> 
> This is a chance to show that the Human Church is divided: ordinary priests and knights can be decent, while Purifiers are becoming dangerous extremists.
> 
> ---
> 
> # NPC OUTCOMES
> 
> ## Purifier Captain Marcen
> 
> If captured alive, Marcen snarls:
> 
> > “You think you have saved them? You have only taught the world to kneel before monsters.”
> 
> He can be:
> - Taken into Church custody by Arlen;
> - Handed to the druids for judgment;
> - Released, if Ekemon wants to demonstrate mercy;
> - Escaped during the collapse, becoming a recurring foe.
> 
> **Best recurring-villain option:** Marcen is injured, loses the hammer, and escapes with a cracked sliver of black iron. He becomes obsessed with proving Ekemon is dangerous.
> 
> ## Dawn-Knight Tomas
> 
> If the party helped him, Tomas becomes a useful future contact inside the Church.
> 
> He is not yet brave enough to rebel openly, but he can:
> - Warn the party about Purifier movements;
> - Smuggle messages from Arlen;
> - Confirm that some Church leaders are being influenced by more radical voices.
> 
> ## Archdruid Lysara
> 
> Lysara becomes a mentor and political ally for Dru and an important moral counterweight for Ekemon.
> 
> She respects strength, but frequently asks the useful question:
> 
> > “Is this power helping you become more yourself—or merely bigger?”
> 
> ---
> 
> # REWARDS
> 
> Give rewards based on the ending, but do not over-stack them. One Titan reward plus one practical item is plenty.
> 
> ## Practical rewards from the druids
> 
> Choose one:
> 
> 1. **Cloak of the Cliff-Walker**  
>    Once per long rest, the wearer can ignore difficult terrain caused by rock, roots, mud, or rubble for 10 minutes.
> 
> 2. **Druidic Stormleaf Tokens (3)**  
>    Crush one as a reaction to gain advantage on a saving throw against lightning, thunder, wind, or falling.
> 
> 3. **Seed of the Scorched Grove**  
>    A small magical seed. If planted in damaged ground and watered, it grows into a sturdy young tree overnight. Once, it can also cast a gentle version of *entangle* in a small area.
> 
> 4. **Sky-Temple Compass**  
>    A bronze-and-crystal compass that points toward the nearest significant Titan relic, storm magic, or ancient sky-temple site. This is excellent if you want the campaign to continue into more Titan adventures.
> 
> ## Social rewards
> 
> - The druids trust Dru, Ekemon, and Varkash.
> - Farmers who hear the story become more open to Ekemon.
> - The Purifiers regard Ekemon as a dangerous Titan-touched enemy.
> - Arlen may become a cautious Church contact rather than an opponent.
> 
> ---
> 
> # GM CHEAT SHEET: WHAT THE PARTY SHOULD LEARN
> 
> By the end of the adventure, make sure they understand these points:
> 
> 1. The **Titan Heartshard** is not inherently evil. It is powerful, frightened, and unstable.
> 2. The **Purifiers** are willing to use destruction in the name of safety and purity.
> 3. The broader **Human Church is not all the same**: Arlen and some Dawn-Knights may be reasonable.
> 4. The druids want balance and protection, but they are also wary of anyone—Ekemon included—holding too much Titan power.
> 5. Ekemon’s Titan Seed is connected to something ancient, and he is being tested by choices rather than merely granted powers.
> 6. Varkash and Dru both have real roles in deciding what kind of hero Ekemon becomes.
> 
> ---
> 
> # TEMPLE MAP: REVEAL-STEP-BY-STEP DESIGN
> 
> For the map, I recommend making the **interior a vertical, side-cutaway dungeon map** rather than a normal top-down floorplan. The temple is built into the canyon wall, and the party is choosing between upper routes that descend to a heart chamber. A side-cutaway makes the split paths intuitive and looks dramatic.
> 
> You can reveal it in this order:
> 
> 1. **Pilgrim Path / broken bridge** — reveal as they approach the temple.
> 2. **Entry Hall and Lightning Carvings** — reveal after crossing the bridge.
> 3. **Upper Sky Temple** — reveal once they open the door.
> 4. **Split Staircase** — reveal the two possible routes but cover their interiors.
> 5. Reveal either **Hall of Thunder** or **Rootway** only when chosen.
> 6. Reveal **Chamber of Broken Weather** after the routes rejoin.
> 7. Reveal the **Heart Chamber** only after the bronze door opens.
> 8. If needed, reveal a simple exterior escape route afterward.
> 
> You may want to print it, draw fog-of-war rectangles over it, or crop the image into sections before showing it to the players.
> 
> ---
> 
> # DALL-E PROMPT: CANYON OF STORMS SKY TEMPLE MAP
> 
> Copy and paste this as one prompt:
> 
> ```text
> Create a high-resolution fantasy tabletop
**user**:
> Okay, please give me the whole Dall-E prompt.
**assistant**:
> ```text
> Create a high-resolution fantasy tabletop roleplaying game dungeon map, designed for a Dungeons & Dragons-style adventure. The map should depict the “Sky Temple of the Canyon of Storms,” an ancient ruined temple carved into the steep wall of a vast canyon. Make it a vertical side-cutaway / cross-section map, not a conventional top-down building plan: show the canyon cliff on the left and the open canyon, waterfall, river far below, and storm clouds on the right. The temple’s rooms and passageways are visibly embedded in the cliff, stacked vertically and connected by stairs, bridges, tunnels, and broken platforms.
> 
> The image must be usable as a player-facing battle map and as a GM reveal map. Do NOT include labels, room names, numbers, grid lines, arrows, legend, text, creatures, people, tokens, or written symbols. Leave distinct rooms and routes visually clear, with good separation and readable architecture. Use a 16:9 landscape composition, with the entire dungeon visible from entrance at the upper left to the final chamber in the lower right. Make paths wide enough for fantasy miniatures and tactical movement. Use a slightly angled side view, like a beautiful old-school RPG cutaway map, but keep floors, platforms, bridges, and room boundaries clear.
> 
> Overall visual style: richly detailed hand-painted fantasy adventure map, polished but readable, dramatic storm lighting, ancient stonework, painterly textures, blue-white lightning, wet rock, moss, roots, waterfalls, and gold-bronze Titan-era architecture. The mood is mysterious, dangerous, elemental, and awe-inspiring. Color palette: stormy slate blue, deep gray rock, blue-white lightning, dark green roots and moss, muted bronze, and occasional warm amber torchlight. No modern objects.
> 
> Include these connected areas, arranged so they can be covered and revealed one section at a time:
> 
> 1. UPPER LEFT: DRUID PILGRIM APPROACH
> A narrow natural cliffside trail emerges from woods and twisted roots at the upper left edge of the image. It approaches a dangerous 20-foot gap across a deep canyon crack. A broken old rope-and-stone bridge spans the gap. The bridge has missing boards, frayed ropes, and windswept stone anchors. Far below, show rocky canyon walls and distant rushing water. This is the party’s approach to the temple.
> 
> 2. UPPER MIDDLE-LEFT: LIGHTNING-CARVING ENTRY CAVE
> Past the broken bridge is a shallow cave entrance in the cliff. The archway is ancient pale stone, carved with a giant humanoid storm figure holding lightning in one hand and a tree in the other. Include a large circular sealed stone door or disk inside the archway, with three clear but non-text symbols: a mountain, a lightning bolt, and a seed. Small arcs of blue-white lightning crawl harmlessly along the carvings. This entrance leads into the temple.
> 
> 3. UPPER CENTER: RUINED SKY TEMPLE ENTRY HALL
> Inside is a broad ruined hall open to the sky through a shattered roof. Include fallen columns, cracked pale stone floors, broken statues of ancient Titan guardians, shallow pools of rainwater, and blue-white magical lightning crawling across the walls. Rain falls through the broken ceiling, but some droplets visibly float upward, making the room strange and magical. At the far side is a broad descending staircase that splits into two routes. Leave generous open floor space for a small combat with storm spirits.
> 
> 4. CENTRAL SPLIT: TWO DESCENDING PATHS
> At the far end of the upper hall, clearly show a split staircase with two separate routes descending through the cliff.
> 
> 4A. LEFT DESCENT: HALL OF THUNDER
> The left route should be visibly faster and more dangerous: a steep stone corridor descending diagonally through the rock. It has metal rods or bronze conductors mounted along both walls, with lightning jumping between them. Include broken floor sections, a narrow ledge, and one exposed dangerous drop where a person could slip. Make it look like a quick, hazardous route for warriors. At its lower end, this route joins a central chamber.
> 
> 4B. RIGHT DESCENT: ROOTWAY OF THE FOURFOLD MOTHER
> The right route should be a slower, natural-looking route: a winding tunnel descending through stone, filled with thick ancient roots, wet walls, green bioluminescent moss, and shallow flowing water. Include a round stone basin filled with glowing rainwater at a small ritual alcove midway down the route. Roots should partly cover an old stone doorway at the end. Make this route peaceful but eerie, strongly connected to druidic nature magic. It also joins the same central chamber.
> 
> 5. LOWER CENTER: CHAMBER OF BROKEN WEATHER
> Both routes emerge into a large circular chamber carved into the cliff. The floor is fragmented into several floating stone platforms that slowly orbit over a deep blue magical storm chasm. Show a narrow but possible route across the floating stones. Rain rises upward from the chasm as mist. The chamber should be tall and dangerous, with a few broken columns and exposed canyon openings. On the far side is a massive sealed bronze double door, decorated with a cracked lightning-bolt emblem and a large palm-shaped indentation. This door leads to the final Heart Chamber.
> 
> 6. LOWER RIGHT: THE HEART CHAMBER
> Beyond the bronze door is the largest and most dramatic chamber. It is carved into the canyon wall but its outer wall has collapsed, leaving the chamber open to the canyon and the enormous storm outside. A waterfall passes near one side of the open chamber, with spray hanging in the air. In the middle floats a large faceted blue-white crystal, about the size of a large backpack or small boulder: the Titan Heartshard. It is suspended above a circular stone platform. Four massive broken stone pillars stand around it at the cardinal directions. Thick chains of magical lightning stretch between the pillars and the crystal. The crystal should pulse with internal light like a heartbeat. Include broken columns and several pieces of cover around the edges for a dramatic confrontation. Make room for a battle between adventurers and human religious soldiers, but do not show any characters.
> 
> 7. OPTIONAL LOWER EXIT / ESCAPE ROUTE
> From the Heart Chamber, show a partially collapsed outer ledge or narrow emergency route leading upward toward the canyon rim, suggesting a possible escape during a temple collapse. It may reconnect visually toward the broken bridge or toward a higher exterior cliff path, but do not make the route too obvious.
> 
> The whole map should make the dungeon’s structure instantly understandable:
> - approach from upper left;
> - entry hall in upper center;
> - clear choice between left lightning route and right root route;
> - both routes reconnect in the floating-platform chamber;
> - final bronze door;
> - dramatic Heartshard chamber at lower right;
> - open canyon, storm clouds, waterfall, and river visible outside the cliff.
> 
> Important constraints:
> - No words, labels, numbers, captions, text, letterforms, map key, or room titles.
> - No grid overlay.
> - No player characters, NPCs, monsters, animals, corpses, or miniatures.
> - No UI elements, borders, parchment frame, logo, watermark, signature, or title.
> - Ensure rooms, paths, bridges, stairs, platforms, and doors are visibly connected and tactically usable.
> - Avoid overly dark areas; every room should be readable.
> - Make the final Heart Chamber the clearest focal point, bright with blue-white lightning against dark storm clouds.
